)\\) and \\((-1,0)\\) satisfy the original equations. Every common point was forced to be one of these two, and their coordinates differ. Thus there are exactly \\(\\boxed{2}\\) distinct common points, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).","solutionAvailability":"present","solutionMethods":[{"directNodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_090","cnm_220"],"explanation":"A common point must satisfy both equations. Multiplying the first equation by \\(4\\) makes its \\(x^2\\) term match the one in the second equation:\n\\[\n4x^2+16y^2=4,\n\\qquad\n4x^2+y^2=4.\n\\]\nSubtracting the second equation from the first eliminates \\(x^2\\) and gives\n\\[\n15y^2=0.\n\\]\nHence \\(y=0\\). Substitution into the original first equation gives \\(x^2=1\\), so \\(x=1\\) or \\(x=-1\\).\n\nBoth \\((1,0)\\) and \\((-1,0)\\) satisfy the original equations. Every common point was forced to be one of these two, and their coordinates differ. Thus there are exactly \\(\\boxed{2}\\) distinct common points, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).","id":"eliminate-the-squared-coordinate","label":"Eliminate the shared squared term","legacyRouteId":"eliminate-the-squared-coordinate","provenance":{"evidence":[{"label":"Accepted primary worked-solution review","path":"../database/knowledge-graph/evidence/solutions-2026-11-06/1966_AMC_12_Q17.json","sha256":"aa3423211f752a149536de862faf33e2e2fff9b0659b70a8179444539eac5646"}],"license":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","licenseURL":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","reviewStatus":"AI-authored and independently AI-reviewed; not teacher reviewed","source":"Original Math Practice solution","sourceRef":"amc/1966_AMC_12_Q17#eliminate-the-squared-coordinate","sourceURL":"https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice"}},{"directNodeIds":["cnm_090","cnm_220"],"id":"squared-coordinate-elimination","label":"Eliminate squared coordinates","legacyRouteId":"squared-coordinate-elimination","provenance":{"evidence":[{"label":"Legacy graph and grading review","path":